<section class="relative min-h-[55vh] flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden bg-[#030a16]"><div class="absolute inset-0 z-0"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782367512/eurocert/m15.png.png" class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-30" alt="ISO 27018 Cloud Privacy"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-[#030a16]/70 via-[#030a16]/40 to-[#030a16]"></div></div><div class="container mx-auto px-6 relative z-10 text-center py-20"><div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-eurogold-400/30 bg-eurogold-400/5 mb-6"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-eurogold-400 animate-pulse"></span><span class="heading-font text-[10px] text-eurogold-400 tracking-[0.3em] uppercase font-bold">Cloud privacy protection</span></div><h1 class="heading-font text-4xl sm:text-6xl md:text-7xl font-bold text-white mb-3 tracking-tight" style="text-shadow:0 0 30px rgba(212,168,67,0.3)">ISO 27018  -  Cloud Privacy</h1><p class="text-white/90 text-sm md:text-base max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">Protecting personally identifiable information in public cloud computing environments.</p></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="grid lg:grid-cols-5 gap-10 items-start"><div class="lg:col-span-3 space-y-6"><div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Standard Overview</h4><h2 class="heading-font text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">ISO 27018  -  <span class="text-euro-600">Cloud Privacy</span></h2></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">To assess applicability, define organizational boundaries, the standard edition, services or activities, locations and intended assurance route before setting an audit scope.</p><div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-euro-100 bg-euro-50/40"><div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3"><div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-euro-600 flex items-center justify-center"><i class="fas fa-info-circle text-white text-xs"></i></div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">What is ISO 27018?</h4></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">ISO/IEC 27018 provides guidance for protecting personally identifiable information (PII) in public cloud environments when a cloud service provider acts as a PII processor. It supplements broader information-security controls with privacy-oriented measures relevant to that role. It does not replace privacy law, make a provider a controller or processor by itself, or establish compliance with every jurisdiction's data-protection requirements.</p></div></div><div class="lg:col-span-2 relative rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-slate-100 h-[320px] md:h-[400px] group"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782367512/eurocert/m15.png.png" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-700 group-hover:scale-105" alt="ISO 27018 Cloud Privacy"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-900/20 via-transparent to-transparent"></div></div></div></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-[#f8fafc]"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="text-center mb-10"><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Discuss the standard, public-cloud processing scope and applicable audit criteria for your organisation.</p><div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#030a16] bg-eurogold-400 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-eurogold-300 transition-all shadow-lg shadow-eurogold-400/20" href="/contact/">Get Started <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-[9px]"></i></a><a href="tel:+919316012883" onclick="return gtag_report_conversion('tel:+919316012883');" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white border border-white/15 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-white/5 transition-all"><i class="fas fa-phone text-[9px]"></i> Call Us Now</a></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white" aria-labelledby="standard-details"><div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-5xl space-y-8"><h2 id="standard-details" class="heading-font text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Applying ISO/IEC 27018 protection of PII in public clouds in practice</h2><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Who should consider it?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Public cloud providers processing customer PII and organisations selecting or overseeing them may use this guidance. It is relevant to outsourced processing where the customer needs clarity about instructions, subprocessors, incidents, access, return or deletion of data and help with data-subject requests. Define services, data categories, processing purposes, regions, provider and customer roles, subcontractors and interfaces. A cloud customer remains responsible for its own controller or processor duties and cannot rely solely on provider documentation.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Key requirements</h3><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed"><li>Identify the PII-processing scope and roles, including services, purposes, data subjects, categories, locations, subprocessors and customer instructions.</li><li>Establish controls for access, confidentiality, processing only on documented instructions, personnel obligations, data return or deletion, and restrictions on provider use of data.</li><li>Provide appropriate transparency and contractual information about processing, locations, subprocessors and relevant changes, while maintaining customer communication and oversight.</li><li>Manage requests, security incidents and breaches, disclosure demands, retention, export and deletion with clear responsibilities and evidence; support customer duties where agreed.</li><li>Monitor and review privacy controls, assess subprocessors, retain evidence, conduct audits and correct gaps. Requirements depend on the cloud role, service and applicable laws, not an automatic compliance checklist.</li></ul></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Certification and audit lifecycle</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">ISO/IEC 27018 is guidance, not a standalone management-system requirements standard. Agree whether it will be assessed as a control extension within ISO/IEC 27001 certification or under another explicit scheme. Where ISO/IEC 27001 certification applies, stage 1 reviews system boundaries, PII processing scope, risk assessment, control applicability and readiness; stage 2 samples operational evidence, contracts, subprocessor oversight, incident handling, customer instructions and deletion or return controls. Findings follow the base or agreed scheme before an independent decision. Surveillance and recertification apply to the underlying certification cycle and include these controls only if stated in scope. Planning depends on processing operations, regions, services, subcontractors, workforce and criteria; no fixed duration is appropriate.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Documents to prepare</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Prepare service and processing scope; PII data-flow maps and role analysis; customer instructions and data-processing agreements; privacy and security risk assessments; subprocessor lists and reviews; data-location and transfer records; access and confidentiality controls; incident and breach logs; retention, return and deletion procedures; request-assistance process; transparency materials; training; control monitoring; internal audit; management review; and corrective actions. Use minimized, secure or redacted samples.</p></div><div><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Before assessing controls, document whether the cloud organization acts as controller, processor or subprocessor for each relevant service and data flow. Agreements should state the customer's instructions, permitted processing, assistance responsibilities, deletion or return expectations and notification channels. A customer's assessment should also consider configuration choices and responsibilities that remain with the customer. Evidence should be current for the named service and region; a general provider statement may not answer every customer-specific question.</p><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Frequently asked questions</h3><div class="space-y-4"><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Who is ISO/IEC 27018 intended for?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Its guidance focuses on public cloud providers processing PII as processors and helps customers understand relevant expectations and shared responsibilities.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does it replace privacy laws?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. Organisations must separately identify and comply with applicable privacy laws and contracts; guidance does not determine legal roles or compliance.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Is it a standalone certification standard?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">It is guidance rather than standalone management-system requirements. Assessment is commonly related to an ISO/IEC 27001 system or another defined scheme.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does a cloud customer need to take action?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. Customers should assess their roles, instructions, configurations, contracts, access, retention and legal duties rather than assume provider controls cover all needs.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What might an audit examine?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Depending on scope, evidence can include processing instructions, contracts, subprocessors, locations, access controls, incident handling, deletion and customer support.</p></article></div></div></div></section>
